WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.402
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.930
RISHI MALIK: Clover is a
point-of-sale device company.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.930
We are the operating system
for small to medium businesses.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.700
That means we make it easy for
coffee shops, for restaurants,

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.630
for retail shops to take
payments, run their business,

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.700
and deliver happiness
to their customers.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.680
Clover's DevOps
philosophy is really

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.110
around self-service for
our engineering teams.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.288
We want to make it as
easy as possible for all

00:00:24.288 --> 00:00:26.580
the amazing engineers and
product managers that we have

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.650
to deliver value
to our customers

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.230
as fast as possible and with
the highest quality possible.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.020
Like many companies, we were
encountering problems of scale.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.430
The way we solved
that problem is

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.770
by adopting more of a
DevOps-first philosophy.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.290
We empowered our
engineering teams

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.220
to build, deploy, and run their
code and provide the tools

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.990
and support they need to do that
effectively and, by doing that,

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.070
removed a lot of the
bottlenecks in our process,

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.195
which means we get code to
production faster, which

00:00:50.195 --> 00:00:51.990
means we get value to
our customers faster.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.880
And they can continue to run
their business in the way

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.755
that they need to in
the changing environment

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:56.580
that we're in.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.890
What we hope to accomplish
by changing our technology

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.570
landscape is to
make sure that we

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.580
can adapt to the
changing environment

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.319
that our customers are in.

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:07.950
We are a company with hundreds
of engineers around the world,

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.990
millions of merchants,
and billions

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.990
of dollars that flow through
our platform in any given year.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.240
That means what we do has
to be well orchestrated,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.592
well planned, and well thought
out so that we do it safely

00:01:18.592 --> 00:01:20.550
and so our customers
don't see any ill effects.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.592
One of the great things
about working with Google

00:01:22.592 --> 00:01:24.450
on our DevOps
transformation has been

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.320
able to utilize the experience
and, frankly, the definition

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.380
of what a lot of this is that
came through Google itself.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.920
We were early in our journey,
and one of the challenges

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.130
that we had was educating
our entire set of engineers

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.010
and all of our teams on what
this DevOps transition and SRE

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:44.010
transition means for
them as individuals.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.470
Google literally
wrote the book on SRE.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:48.000
But the Google
support allowed us

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
to bring in Google SREs
and Google DevOps experts

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.860
to train the rest of our teams.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:55.380
And that meant that we could
accomplish a lot more a lot

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.067
faster.

00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:58.650
Having gone through this makes
me very grateful for the people

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.350
that I have around me.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.860
Specifically, I'd love to thank
all of the engineers at Clover

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.840
for going through this
transition with us,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.132
all of the product managers,
all of our customers.

00:02:09.132 --> 00:02:10.590
Most importantly,
all of the people

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.210
that are really living
and breathing this,

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.835
the people who are
putting in the hours

00:02:13.835 --> 00:02:17.820
doing documentation, the people
who are writing code and making

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.500
changes and going
above and beyond what

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.750
their normal jobs are, just
because they believe in what

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.750
we're doing, and they
know this is the best path

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:24.720
forward for us.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.390
It's not a simple thing, and
it takes a lot of effort.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:29.190
And I'm very
grateful for everyone

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.420
who's helped out with that.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

